# Aero Engine — LeakSanitizer suppressions (task 0.4.2, spec D16: evidence-gated, never pre-seeded).
#
# Scope: wired ONLY into the Debug ctest step's LSAN_OPTIONS for Linux (.github/workflows/ci.yml) —
# the one lane where LSan runs. First-party leak detection stays fully ON: every entry below names a
# specific THIRD-PARTY allocation origin observed on the first GPU-backed CI run (PR #16, run
# 29599267631). There, doctest reported 227/227 SUCCESS and LSan then flagged ~3.1 MB in 57,584
# allocations, all rooted in SDL3's x11 video-driver init and SDL_GPU's Vulkan probe
# (lavapipe/loader frames) — zero allocation sites in engine/ or tests/ code; engine frames appear
# only as mid-stack call sites (Context::Context -> SDL_Init, Device::create ->
# SDL_CreateGPUDeviceWithProperties). Surfaced now because 0.4.2's gated tests are the first thing
# on this lane to init a REAL video driver (x11 under Xvfb); the pre-0.4.2 suite was dummy-driver
# only and never touched these paths.
#
# Rules for this file (spec D16): add an entry ONLY with observed CI evidence, one commented entry
# per observed frame; never suppress module-wide (e.g. leak:SDL) — that would blind LSan to real
# engine leaks whose allocations happen inside SDL (a forgotten SDL_ReleaseGPU* would allocate
# under SDL_CreateGPUBuffer et al., which stay unsuppressed).
#
# The two lavapipe entries at the bottom (leak:libvulkan_lvp, leak:libLLVM) are deliberate,
# narrowly-justified exceptions to "never module-wide". They are admissible where leak:SDL is not,
# for two reasons: (1) their leak frames NEVER symbolize to a function/source name — the driver
# stack is unmapped before exit, so the module path is the ONLY matchable token (see those entries
# and tests/vulkan_stack_pin.cpp); and (2) they do not create a realistic first-party blind spot:
# every engine GPU resource is pool-tracked and released by ~Device before LSan runs (the "~Device
# releasing N leaked ..." safety net), so no engine leak survives inside the driver at exit, and
# CPU-side engine leaks never touch these modules at all (leak:libLLVM in particular can only ever
# match Mesa's shader-codegen backend, which no engine allocation path reaches). If a future
# dbgsym-enabled run yields the specific worker-thread FUNCTION name, tighten to that name and drop
# the module scope.

# SDL3's x11 video driver retains keyboard/XKB tables per init cycle (SDL_x11keyboard.c:166/:177).
leak:X11_InitKeyboard
# SDL3's x11 video init retains XRandR mode / content-scale data (SDL_x11modes.c, reached from
# X11_VideoInit, SDL_x11video.c:448 — this entry covers those stacks via any-frame matching).
leak:X11_VideoInit
# SDL_GPU's Vulkan backend probe retains loader/lavapipe allocations from instance/physical-device
# enumeration (SDL_gpu_vulkan.c:12362/:12709), reached via both VULKAN_PrepareDriver and
# VULKAN_CreateDevice.
leak:VULKAN_INTERNAL_DeterminePhysicalDevice
leak:VULKAN_INTERNAL_PrepareVulkan

# Mesa/lavapipe worker-thread pool (task 0.5.2). The first texture-sampling fragment shader the cube
# test compiles+runs spins up a fixed lavapipe pool (one thread per core = 4 on the CI runner); each
# worker calloc's a ~56-byte per-thread struct never freed at exit (224 B / 4 allocations). NOT the
# rasterizer pool (LP_NUM_THREADS=0 left the count unchanged, run 29679720635) and NOT the disk-cache
# writer pool (MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DISABLE likewise, run 29680407602) — a different, non-disableable
# pool. SDL loads/UNLOADS libvulkan.so.1 per Device lifecycle, and this Ubuntu loader unmaps the ICD
# (libvulkan_lvp.so) + its libLLVM with it, so by teardown they read <unknown module> at LSan-time;
# LD_PRELOAD to keep them mapped is impossible (aborts the ASan runtime), and pinning only the loader
# did not stop the ICD unmap (run 29681929403). So tests/vulkan_stack_pin.cpp dlopens the ICD ITSELF
# with RTLD_NODELETE IN-PROCESS — that mapping then survives every dlclose, so the frames resolve to
# real module names and these two entries match (LSan matches suppressions against function/file/
# module names). The pool lives in libvulkan_lvp.so and/or its libLLVM codegen backend (the leak split
# across two load bases with identical offsets); both are covered — a still-red run would NAME the true
# module in the log. Runs 29679001505 / 29679720635 / 29680407602 / 29681071731 / 29681929403. See the
# module-wide-exception rationale in the header.
leak:libvulkan_lvp
leak:libLLVM

# The assimp port's own loaders leak on their ERROR paths (task 3.2.5, run 31559183254 — the Linux
# Debug lane; macOS and Windows passed the SAME commit, and no local run can see this at all, since
# LSan runs on that one lane). doctest reported 297,194 assertions / 0 failed and LSan then flagged
# 4704 B in 17 allocations. Every record is rooted in one of two library functions that allocate
# through raw owning pointers and then throw DeadlyImportError, freeing nothing on the way out:
#
#   * ColladaParser's CONSTRUCTOR — 15 of the 17. It parses the whole document from the ctor body
#     (ColladaParser.cpp:464 -> ReadContents -> ReadStructure), so a document it rejects throws
#     BEFORE the object exists: ~ColladaParser never runs, and every Collada::Node / Collada::Mesh /
#     SubMesh / InputChannel / Transform already allocated is orphaned. Observed allocation sites,
#     each under that ctor: ColladaParser.cpp:1370 (ReadGeometryLibrary), :2097 (ReadSceneLibrary),
#     :2118 (ReadSceneNode), plus the container-growth frames beneath them.
#   * STLImporter::LoadASCIIFile — the other 2, STLLoader.cpp:240 and :244, the position/normal
#     arrays for a file truncated mid-record.
#
# Surfaced now because this task's failure-mode fixtures are the first things on this lane to drive
# those loaders into their error paths AT ALL: tests/editor/assimp_import_test.cpp:977 (AI9, the
# truncated .stl/.ply) and :1890 (the cross-document <instance_geometry url="other.dae#g1"/>). The
# defect is upstream — the port is consumed unmodified, since this tree does not patch third-party
# sources — so the fixtures stay exactly as they are; they are the coverage, not the bug.
#
# Frame-scoped, and NEVER module-wide: leak:assimp / leak:libassimp would blind LSan to a first-party
# leak allocated anywhere inside the library, which is the same reason leak:SDL is refused above.
# These two entries cannot create that blind spot, because nothing of ours allocates inside either
# frame: the only first-party code the library can call from there is RefusingIoSystem's twelve
# IOSystem virtuals (editor/src/assimp_import.cpp), every one of which answers "cannot" without
# allocating — Open returns nullptr, CurrentDirectory returns a function-local static empty string.
# Our converters run after the library has returned, in our own frames, which stay unsuppressed.
# A future format added to that backend may reach further loaders that leak the same way; each needs
# its own entry, gated on its own observed CI evidence and naming its own frame — never a widened one.
leak:Assimp::ColladaParser::ColladaParser
leak:Assimp::STLImporter::LoadASCIIFile

# stb_vorbis leaks on ITS OWN error paths when a codebook is SPARSE (task 3.7.1, run 32584140126,
# job 97057871012 on PR #88 — the Linux Debug lane; macOS and Windows passed the SAME commit, and no
# local run can see this at all, since LSan runs on that one lane). doctest reported 301,543
# assertions / 0 failed and LSan then flagged 632 B in 1 allocation.
#
# The mechanism, read out of the pinned source (stb_vorbis v1.22, vcpkg's stb port):
#
#   :3739-3742  if (c->sparse) lengths = setup_temp_malloc(f, c->entries);
#               else           lengths = c->codeword_lengths = setup_malloc(f, c->entries);
#
# In the SPARSE arm `lengths` is a LOCAL and nothing else; only the non-sparse arm also parks it on
# the codebook, where vorbis_deinit could find it. We pass alloc_buffer = nullptr (correctly — an
# arena would have to be sized by guesswork, and too small a one refuses legitimate input), so
# setup_temp_malloc (:969-978) is a plain malloc rather than a bump allocation. The two matching
# setup_temp_free calls sit at :3780 and :3840, both on the success path, and start_decoder has four
# error returns between the allocation and the first of them (:3752, :3753, :3765, :3778) — so a
# stream that is rejected mid-codebook orphans the buffer. 632 B is (entries + 7) & ~7 for a codebook
# of 625..632 entries.
#
# NOTHING OF OURS CAN CLOSE IT: stb_vorbis_open_memory returns nullptr on that path, so the
# unique_ptr guard in decodeWithStbVorbis (editor/src/audio_decode.cpp:267) is never constructed —
# there is no handle to own. Our refusal is correct and the leak is upstream. The port is consumed
# unmodified, since this tree does not patch third-party sources, so the fixture stays exactly as it
# is: AD14 (tests/editor/audio_decode_test.cpp:309, the first 40 % of each of the four fixtures) is
# the COVERAGE, not the bug. Surfaced now because AD14 is the first thing on this lane to drive an
# Ogg Vorbis header into its rejection path at all.
#
# Bounded: one failed open, one allocation, no loop — and in the editor it is reachable only from a
# corrupt or truncated .ogg, which is refused rather than decoded.
#
# Frame-scoped, and NEVER module-wide: leak:stb_vorbis would blind LSan to a first-party leak
# allocated anywhere inside the library, which is the same reason leak:SDL is refused in the header
# above and leak:assimp is refused for the entry above this one. This entry cannot create that blind
# spot, because nothing of ours allocates inside start_decoder: we hand stb_vorbis_open_memory a byte
# span and a null allocator, and it calls back into no code of ours. Our own conversion runs after
# the library has returned, in our own frames, which stay unsuppressed. A future error path in a
# different stb_vorbis function that leaks the same way needs its OWN entry, gated on its own
# observed CI evidence and naming its own frame — never a widened one.
#
# A LIMITATION OF THIS ENTRY, RECORDED RATHER THAN FIXED (task 3.7.1's code-review round). Unlike the
# two Assimp:: entries above, `start_decoder` is an UNQUALIFIED C static — stb_vorbis is a C library
# with no namespace and the function has internal linkage — so this token is NOT library-unique. A
# future dependency carrying a same-named static frame would inherit this suppression silently, with
# nothing in LSan's output saying so. There is no narrower spelling available: LSan matches
# suppressions against function, file and module names and has no "this function in this translation
# unit" form, and both alternatives are worse — a module scope (leak:stb_vorbis) is exactly what the
# paragraph above refuses, and dropping the entry reddens the Linux Debug lane on an upstream defect
# this tree does not patch. If a same-named frame ever does appear, the tell is a leak that stops
# being reported without anyone having fixed it, and the answer is to re-derive the suppression from
# a fresh LSan report rather than to widen this one.
leak:start_decoder
